@page "/docs/components/drag-drop"

<Seo Canonical="/docs/components/drag-drop" Title="Blazorise DropZone system" Description="Learn to use and work with the Blazorise DropZone component which allows you to easily drag and drop any component or HTML element." />

<DocsPageTitle Path="Components/Drop Zone" AddedVersion="Added in v1.0">
    Blazorise Drag &amp; Drop component
</DocsPageTitle>

<DocsPageLead>
    Quickly drag and drop elements between the containers.
</DocsPageLead>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    The Drag &amp; Drop is comprised of <Code>DropContainer</Code> and <Code>DropZone</Code> components. The <Code>DropContainer</Code> is used as a container for all items that are being dragged,
    along with the <Code>DropZone</Code>'s that are basically an areas to drag the items.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<UnorderedList>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Paragraph>
            <Code Tag>DropContainer</Code> the main container.
        </Paragraph>
        <UnorderedList>
            <UnorderedListItem>
                <Paragraph>
                    <Code Tag>DropZone</Code> wrapper for drop zone content.
                </Paragraph>
            </UnorderedListItem>
        </UnorderedList>
    </UnorderedListItem>
</UnorderedList>



<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Examples
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Basic">
        <Paragraph>
            To start, first define a <Code>DropContainer</Code> and assign the <Code>Items</Code> to it.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Next you define <Code>DropZone</Code>'s and assign them a unique <Code>Name</Code>. The <Code>Name</Code>, along with the <Code>ItemsFilter</Code> parameter is used to determine to which <Code>DropZone</Code> an item will belong.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The callback <Code>ItemDropped</Code> is used to update the data item, when a drag operation has finished.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="BasicDragDropExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <BasicDragDropExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Reorder items">
        <Paragraph>
            Items can be reordered inside each dropzone with the <Code>AllowReorder</Code> bool set to true on the dropzone.
            The dragged item leaves a slot that moves with the insertion point, with or without animation.
            The <Code>Reordered</Code> function triggers each time the order changes or items are added or removed from the dropzone, providing the current order in a dictionary.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="DragDropReorderingExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <DragDropReorderingExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Animated reordering">
        <Paragraph>
            Animated movement helps users follow where items will land as they reorder a list. Enable <Code>Animated</Code> together with <Code>AllowReorder</Code> on a <Code>DropZone</Code> to animate neighboring items around the insertion placeholder.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Set <Code>AnimationDuration</Code> to control the movement duration in milliseconds. The default is <Code>200</Code>. Animations are disabled by default, and a duration of zero or less disables them. The user's reduced motion preference is respected automatically.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Set <Code>ShowPlaceholder="true"</Code> to show a dashed outline or your <Code>PlaceholderTemplate</Code> inside the moving slot. Set it to <Code>false</Code> to hide the outline and content while retaining the slot's space.
            The default, <Code>null</Code>, shows the placeholder for non-animated reordering or when a custom template is supplied; otherwise, it leaves an empty slot. Use the controls below to compare visibility and animation independently.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="DragDropAnimationExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <DragDropAnimationExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Custom placeholder">
        <Paragraph>
            Use <Code>PlaceholderTemplate</Code> to replace the default reorder indicator with custom content. The template receives the item being dragged and can be defined on the <Code>DropContainer</Code> or overridden by an individual <Code>DropZone</Code>.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            This example places the zones in equal responsive columns so that longer placeholder content and changes in item count do not resize the zones during dragging.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="DragDropCustomPlaceholderExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <DragDropCustomPlaceholderExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Best Practices
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Drag Alternative
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Every operation available through dragging should also have a keyboard-accessible method, such as move buttons. Make draggable items and valid drop zones visually clear, and show immediate feedback for both allowed and rejected drops.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="DragDropAccessibleBestPracticeExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <DragDropAccessibleBestPracticeExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Validate Every Move
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Use stable item identifiers and update the source collection through one state owner after a successful drop. When ordering, permissions, or destination rules affect persisted data, validate the move again in application code before saving it.
</DocsPageParagraph>

